1. Scope and definitions

"Covered Material" means the original project-authored source, models,
documentation, tests, and build/deployment scripts in tools/price-optimization
distributed with this license, and modifications or derivative works of that
material. A copy remains covered when moved, renamed, embedded, or translated.
Expressly identified third-party material, previously MIT-licensed material,
and files or portions expressly offered under other terms by their copyright
holders are excluded and retain their own licenses. Downstream recipients
cannot remove these conditions by relabeling Covered Material. This license
does not revoke any permission already granted for an earlier version under
another license.

"Licensor" means each copyright holder offering its Covered Material under
these terms. "You" means the person or entity exercising permission under this
license. Acting for an entity requires authority to bind that entity.

"Covered Source" is the preferred editable form of Covered Material, including
your covered changes, models, interface definitions, and scripts needed to
build and install it. It excludes independently licensed third-party programs,
general-purpose system tools, credentials, personal data, and operational data.
Provide documented configuration schemas and non-sensitive sample values so
that excluding those items does not conceal covered implementation changes.

"Deployment" includes running Covered Material yourself or having it run for
you, in a local process, library, service, hosted system, or other arrangement.
Using a separate application or a human intermediary to apply the resulting
recommendations does not remove your Deployment from these conditions.

"Productive Deployment" means a Deployment used to make, recommend, evaluate,
or automate actual prices, production, allocation, work, or other operational
decisions for a store, factory, service, or other productive unit. Offline
education, development, and research that do not direct or evaluate actual
operational decisions are not Productive Deployments. Calling an operational
deployment a pilot, experiment, nonprofit activity, or advisory service does
not create an exception.

"Workers" are the people who regularly perform the labor of the productive
unit, including part-time, temporary, agency, and contract workers and workers
on protected leave. Employment labels must not be used to deny these rights.
"Net Surplus" is the unit's revenue after necessary, reasonable operating
costs, fair labor compensation, taxes, and democratically approved reserves.
"Essentials" are goods or services needed for basic nutrition, drinking water,
shelter, sanitation, necessary healthcare, basic energy, or access to those
goods and services.

2. Conditional permission

Subject to all these terms, each Licensor grants you a worldwide, nonexclusive,
royalty-free copyright license to use, run, reproduce, study, modify, display,
distribute, and host its Covered Material, including charging for compliant
copies, work, or services. A Licensor also grants the patent rights it controls
that are necessarily infringed by its contributed Covered Material alone.
No rights to trademarks, third-party software, or third-party patents are
granted. All grants remain effective while you comply and may terminate only
as provided here. No payment buys an exception to these conditions.

Sections 3 through 6 are conditions of these grants to the extent permitted
by applicable law, and obligations you undertake when accepting this license.
No permission is needed for rights that applicable law gives you independently
of this license. You may decline these terms and refrain from exercising the
permissions they offer.

3. Prohibited uses and facilitation

You must not use Covered Material, or intentionally or knowingly supply,
configure, support, or operate it for another party, to do any of the following:

(a) Enable slavery, trafficking, debt bondage, involuntary labor under threat
    of a penalty, confiscation of identity documents to prevent departure, or
    the withholding of earned pay to compel continued labor. Consent must be
    informed, freely given, and withdrawable without coercion. Prohibited
    conduct remains prohibited even if a local authority permits it.

(b) Coerce workers through deprivation of essentials, threats to immigration
    status or family, unlawful confinement, or punitive manipulation of work
    or pay; suppress collective organizing or lawful collective action;
    retaliate against reporting harm, exercising the rights in section 4,
    refusing dangerous work, or challenging an automated decision; or use
    surveillance to identify, intimidate, or punish people for those acts.

(c) Set or coordinate prices, access, or allocations that disadvantage people
    because of race, ethnicity, caste, religion, sex, gender, sexual orientation,
    disability, age, nationality, migration status, or protected labor activity,
    including through intentionally selected proxies. Fair, transparent
    assistance that improves access for disadvantaged people is permitted.

(d) Price Essentials by exploiting an individual's distress, dependence,
    inability to leave, lack of alternatives, or inferred willingness to pay;
    manufacture scarcity by withholding usable essential stock to increase
    prices; or raise essential prices during an emergency beyond documented
    increases in reasonable supply and operating costs. Price differences
    must have a documented cost, accessibility, or need-based justification.

(e) Identify, track, rank, or target people for persecution, arbitrary detention,
    violence, or denial of fundamental rights; perform indiscriminate monitoring
    of people's private lives; or enable torture, physical abuse, or the worst
    forms of child labor, including hazardous or coerced child labor.

You must not deliberately avoid clear evidence of these uses or use affiliates,
contractors, subprocesses, or intermediaries to evade these conditions. Upon
credible evidence of a prohibited use in a Deployment you control or support,
promptly investigate, stop the affected use or support, preserve non-sensitive
evidence, and apply section 7. Merely publishing a compliant general-purpose
copy does not impose a duty to identify or surveil every unknown recipient.

4. Worker control and benefit in Productive Deployments

Before and throughout a Productive Deployment, you must ensure that each
productive unit whose operations it serves has enforceable written rules and
actual practices satisfying all of the following:

(a) Workers exercise final democratic control, with equal voting rights per
    worker, over deployment objectives, pricing and access constraints,
    compensation policy, allocation of Net Surplus, and appointment and removal
    of those entrusted with operational authority. Workers may delegate routine
    decisions but can recall delegates and change or stop the Deployment.
    Capital ownership, a founder, a platform, or an outside investor must not
    confer a veto or override these worker decisions.

(b) Net Surplus belongs collectively to the Workers, who decide its allocation
    to labor-based distributions, reserves, reinvestment, and community benefit.
    No passive owner, shareholder, founder, licensor, or related party may take
    dividends, equity appreciation realized from the unit's surplus, profit
    shares, or equivalent extraction merely for owning or controlling capital.
    Genuine, documented operating purchases and reasonable fixed financing
    costs are permitted; sham rent, debt, management fees, or other transfers
    used to disguise extraction are not. Financing may not transfer control.

(c) Workers have timely access to understandable objectives, constraints,
    decision records, financial accounts, and surplus allocations, with privacy
    safeguards. They can contest results and obtain review by accountable
    people with power to pause, reverse, or remedy a decision. A software
    system must not be the sole unaccountable authority over these rights.

(d) Work is voluntary and fairly compensated, at least to applicable lawful
    wage and safety standards. A democratic vote cannot waive an individual's
    protections in section 3 or authorize unsafe or coercive conditions.

Legal title may be held by a cooperative, trust, association, or other suitable
entity; its substance must satisfy these conditions. They apply to the units
using the Deployment, not automatically to unrelated activities. A managed
optimization service is itself a productive unit, and the client units served
by its operational recommendations must also comply. Obtain their written
commitment before operational service and stop serving a noncompliant use.

5. Copies, derivatives, and hosted access

Every distributed copy of Covered Material must retain copyright and
attribution notices, include this complete license, clearly identify covered
files, and identify your modifications and their dates. Each recipient receives
the grants directly from the relevant Licensors under these same terms.
License your copyrightable changes to Covered Material under this exact version
of these terms; you may not offer those changes under weaker alternative terms
or impose terms that prevent recipients from exercising the rights here.

With any distributed executable or other non-source form, provide its complete
Covered Source under these terms through an accompanying copy or a working,
clearly identified, no-charge download. Access to that source may not require
an additional contract, non-disclosure promise, or purchase. Keep the download
available for at least three years after your last distribution of that build.

If you modify Covered Material and make it available for interaction over a
network, including through a separate service, offer every interacting user
and the Workers of affected productive units the Covered Source of the running
version without charge. Put an accessible source link in the service interface
or documented API and give Workers a usable copy or link. Keep the source
available throughout that service and for three years after it ends. Sensitive
data and credentials must be excluded as defined in section 1.

Independent works that merely invoke the engine through an interface can keep
their own licenses, including MIT. Aggregating or connecting them does not
relicense them, and does not waive these terms for the Covered Material or
your Deployment. Outputs consisting only of prices, facts, or other data are
not Covered Material merely because the engine generated them; your use of
the engine to generate operational outputs is still subject to these terms.
Do not distribute a combination if another component's terms prevent you
from meeting these obligations. This license grants no right to redistribute
AMPL, its SDK, solver binaries, license files, or other third-party material.

6. Accountable and privacy-respecting deployment

For a Productive Deployment, maintain an accurate deployment notice identifying
the responsible operator, a worker-accessible contact for concerns, the engine
version, the applicable license, and a plain-language description of its purpose.
Make this notice accessible to Workers and service users. Keep written records
of worker governance, approved objectives and constraints, material changes,
aggregate surplus allocation, and responses to substantiated harm reports for
the deployment's duration and two years afterward, subject to mandatory law.
Give Workers an understandable report on these matters at least annually.

Upon a Licensor's written request explaining specific evidence of a material
breach, provide, within 30 days, a good-faith written explanation supported by
relevant, appropriately redacted records, or explain any lawful impediment
and provide an aggregate or independent verification alternative. No routine
telemetry, access to your systems, public reporting of individual records, or
identification of complainants is required or authorized by this provision.
Do not collect unnecessary personal data to prove compliance. Protect workers
and complainants from retaliation and disclosure that could expose them to harm.

7. Breach, cessation, cure, and remedies

Your grants terminate automatically upon a material breach of sections 3 or 4.
Stop the affected Deployment immediately and cease other exercises of these
grants. Following such a breach, reinstatement requires the affected Licensor's
express written permission after cessation, correction, and a documented plan
to prevent recurrence and address harm. Affected people retain their independent
legal rights; reinstatement does not settle or waive their claims.

For a breach confined to notice, source delivery, or recordkeeping under
sections 5 or 6, you have 30 days from discovering it or receiving written
notice, whichever is earlier, to fully correct it. Your grants terminate if
you fail to do so. A first such breach corrected within that period does not
terminate the grants. This cure period never authorizes continued prohibited
conduct or denial of worker control.

After termination you may retain an inactive archival copy solely as required
by law, to preserve evidence, or to prepare a cure; no productive operation or
further distribution is permitted without reinstatement. Compliant downstream
recipients do not lose their own grants because of your breach. Accrued
obligations, retained notices, confidentiality of protected records, and
liability for past breaches survive termination. Licensors may seek remedies
available under applicable law, including an injunction or damages where a
court permits them. No automatic penalty or guaranteed remedy is created.

